  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n136 PM Update...\n\nA severe thunderstorm watch is in effect in til 8 PM tonight.\nThis is for all of our southeastern Ohio and a majority of our \nWest Virginia counties, basically the I-64 corridor and north. \nLincoln, Boone, and Raleigh are also included. Seeing generous \nconvection forming across our western forecast area with \ndownburst wind gusts reaching the 40-50 mph range as reported by\na few ASOS sites. Expecting these storms to hold together and \neven strengthen as they traverse the area ESEward. \n\nThe best wind dynamics will be situated across SE'rn Ohio and\nnorthern West Virginia (especially the US-50 corridor) where\nbetter wind shear (20-40 knots) is present. Rapid clearing is\noccurring over the central lowlands and coalfields which will\nbuild better instability through the afternoon. This will be\nanother area to watch later this afternoon and evening.\n\n1204 PM Update... \nFlash flooding concerns continue through tonight. Additional \nrounds of showers and thunderstorms will move across the area \nthis afternoon and evening leading to additional or worsening \nflooding. ~12z HRRR shows the next round forming to our west in \nOhio and subsequently moving west to east across our area later \nthis afternoon and evening. The ~12z NAM is concerning as it \nshows the next round forming over Ohio and then tracking across \nour area between 1 PM and 3 PM, offering only a small break \nbetween our most recent deluge. It then shows another hefty \nround later this evening through tonight. \n\nSome of these storms could be on the strong to severe side \ncontaining damaging wind gusts and of course heavy downpours. CAPE \nis expected to be between 2,500 and 3,500 J/kg if timely clearing \noccurs this afternoon. This is more than sufficient to spawn \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms, with scattered strong to \nsevere storms being a possibility. \n\nThere is the chance for a tornado or two to occur with any more \norganized, clear-air storms. The main driver for this threat would \nbe if an additional surface low forms along the stationary front \ncurrently bisecting the area. This would cause a backing in the wind \nfield increasing wind shear from 20-30 knots to at least 40-60 \nknots. Otherwise, damaging downburst winds and the heavy rainfall \nwill be the main concerns. \n\nCurrent guidance suggests that at least another 1-2 inches of \nrainfall will fall across the area through tonight. Locally higher \namounts between 3-5 inches could be possible somewhere in Ohio or \ncentral/northern WV if the NAM solution is right. A flood watch goes \nuntil 8 AM Monday to cover the likely additional flash flooding\nlater this afternoon through tonight. A rare moderate risk \n(level 3/4) for excessive rainfall spans the bulk of WV with the\nrest of the area in a slight risk (level 2/4).\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are expected again for Monday, but the\nsevere threat and primary heavy rain threat shifts south of our\narea as a cold front crosses. A marginal risk (level 1/4) for \nexcessive rainfall is still in place across our area for Monday\nwith a marginal risk (level 1/5) for severe weather covers our\nVA and far southern WV counties.\n\nA bit of a reprieve is expected behind this front for Tuesday\nand Wednesday until another front crosses Thursday.",
